Spatial sweep of mlir-aie ml/bottleneck (int8) vs CPU ORT int8 -- does NPU GOPS scale?
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07, both sides one sitting.

  NPU : kernels/bottleneck_sweep/sweep.py     (mlir-aie ironenv, py3.13)
  CPU : kernels/bottleneck_sweep/cpu_sweep.py (resnet_env, onnxruntime 1.22.1)

THE QUESTION THIS ANSWERS
=========================
results/aie/conv2x_int8_cpu_baseline.log closed exactly one design at one shape -- the
chained 3-bottleneck ml/resnet/layers_conv2_x at 32x32x256, losing to Zen4 CPU ORT int8
by 6.3-8.5x -- and named the experiment that would widen or narrow it:

    "running standalone `ml/bottleneck` at 32x32 against a larger spatial and watching
     whether GOPS scales is the experiment that would turn 'this design at this shape
     loses' into either 'this op class loses' or 'this op class needs spatial >= N'."

That experiment is this log. Answer: the op class loses. Throughput does climb with
size, and it climbs nowhere near far enough -- the CPU is ahead by at least 5.7x at
every shape, rising to 7.6x (hardware bracket) / 11.4x (end to end) at 32x32. The 5.7x
is the FLOOR of the gap, measured at the single shape most favourable to the NPU and
least favourable to the CPU; it is not the typical case.

WHICH AXIS WAS SWEPT, AND WHY
=============================
tensor_h is the free axis; tensor_w is not. Every L1 buffer in bottleneck.py is sized
tensor_w * channels bytes (l1_in_ty is (w,1,256) int8) while rows stream through
range_(tensor_h) one at a time. So h scales the problem at constant memory and w does
not. This was predicted from the source and then confirmed by the compile failures
below, which are all width failures and none of them height failures.

HOW THE VERDICT IS DECIDED -- the fit, not per-point GOPS
=========================================================
Per-point end-to-end GOPS *must* climb with size on any accelerator, because the largely
fixed host cost (447.3us host intercept, results/aie/dispatch_floor_npu.log; see point 4
for how far the measured cost here exceeds it) shrinks as a fraction of a bigger job. Reading that rise as "the array scales" would be a measurement artifact. The honest
metric is a least-squares fit of

    hw_time = intercept + slope * FLOPs      ->   marginal GOPS = 1/slope

which is the array's steady-state throughput with every fixed cost removed. Both sides
are fit the same way, from the same FLOP formula, over the same shape list.

CORRECTNESS GATE
================
Every NPU shape is checked against the torch int8 golden from mlir-aie's own
ml/bottleneck/test.py (same scales, same DataShaper layouts: YCXC8/CYX in,
OIYXI8O8/OIYX weights, CDYX/YCXD out) before its timings are kept. All five verified
shapes passed. No shape entered the fit unverified.

