WHAT THIS SHOWS
===============
1. GOPS DOES scale -- and not nearly enough. NPU hardware throughput rises monotonically
   from 116.7 to 143.7 GOPS over a 16x increase in work, a 23% gain that is flattening
   (the last doubling bought 2%). Marginal GOPS, 146.1, is the asymptote the per-point
   column is converging to. So 32x32 was mildly underutilizing the array, and fixing
   that entirely would move the verdict from 7.6x to 5.7x. It does not change it.

2. The gap survives at the shape most favourable to the NPU. 512x32 is simultaneously
   the NPU's best point (143.7 GOPS, 98% of its asymptote) and the CPU's worst (820.0
   GOPS, its only sub-900 row -- the working set has outgrown cache there). Even at that
   pairing the CPU is 5.7x faster on the hardware bracket and 6.0x end-to-end. Every
   other shape is worse for the NPU.

3. Marginal GOPS: 146.1 (NPU) vs 819.0 (CPU) = 5.6x, matching the per-shape ratio at the
   large end. Both routes to the number agree, which is what makes this a verdict on the
   op class rather than on a shape.

4. Host cost is large, and it is NOT the flat floor -- a correction to how this repo has
   been reading that number. The host ms column here is wall - hw, so the like-for-like
   quantity in results/aie/dispatch_floor_npu.log is that log's HOST intercept, 447.3us,
   not its 617.0us wall intercept (617.0 = 447.3 host + 169.8 hardware). Measured here:

       32x32   608us     128x32  832us     512x32  874us
       64x32   695us     256x32  812us

   That is 160-430us ABOVE the 447.3us floor and it grows with payload -- a 44% rise
   across a 16x increase in bytes moved, consistent with buffer sync being charged
   outside the hardware bracket in proportion to size. It is not flat, and it is not a
   reproduction of a fixed cost.

   This also corrects results/aie/conv2x_int8_cpu_baseline.log, which called its 628.2us
   host cost a reproduction of "617.0us to ~2%". Those are different quantities: 628.2
   host against a 447.3us host floor is 40% over, not 2% under. The agreement was
   numerology. That log is left as written (results logs are never rewritten here); this
   is the correction. Nothing in either verdict depends on it -- dispatch was never the
   thing to fix, and the reason is point 2, not this arithmetic.

   Where the fixed cost does matter is the amortization argument, which survives intact:
   host cost is 33% of wall time at 32x32 and 5% at 512x32, and removing it entirely
   still leaves the 5.7x hardware-bracket loss.

5. tensor_w = 32 is a hard ceiling for this design, and it is a structural one. 56x56,
   32x64, 64x64 and 128x64 all fail identically in aiecc, not at runtime:

       aie.mlir:2:3: error: 'aie.tile' op allocated buffers exceeded available memory
       aie.mlir:2:3: error: 'aie.tile' op Basic sequential allocation also failed.
       MemoryMap: (stack) 2560 B, outOFL2L3_buff_0 16384, outOFL2L3_buff_1 16384,
                  wts_buf_02_cons_buff_0 16384, skip_buf_cons_buff_0 16384,
                  skip_buf_cons_buff_1 16384

   The skip-add core, Tile(0,4), needs five w*256-byte buffers plus a 2560-byte stack
   against 64 KB of AIE2 tile local memory. At w=64 that is 5 x 16384 + 2560 = 84480 B;
   at w=56 the buffers shrink to 14336 B each (confirmed in the failure dump:
   memref<56x1x256xi8>) for 74240 B, still over. Bank-aware allocation fails first,
   sequential allocation fails after it -- 8 error lines for 4 shapes, two per shape, all
   on tile(0,4). Widening this design past w=32 requires restructuring its buffering, not
   a parameter change.

   (The memtile also logs a bank-aware failure for the 69632-byte weights buffer at every
   shape including the five that pass; sequential allocation covers it there. It is a
   warning, not the cause.)

   Consequence worth stating plainly: ResNet50's real conv2_x is 56x56, and this design
   cannot compile that shape at all. The 32x32 that layers_conv2_x runs -- and that
   results/aie/conv2x_int8_cpu_baseline.log measured -- is not the network's shape; it
   is the largest square that fits.

6. The CPU fit is the weaker of the two numbers, deliberately reported anyway. r^2 is
   0.98870 with a NEGATIVE intercept (-116.9us), which is not physical: the CPU's
   per-FLOP cost is not constant in problem size, because cache behaviour changes (GOPS
   peaks near 1094 at 32x64 and falls to 820 at 512x32). A straight line through that is
   an approximation. The NPU fit has no such problem (r^2 = 0.99999, physical +270.0us
   intercept). So the per-shape table above is the primary evidence and the CPU marginal
   GOPS is corroboration -- but both point the same way, and the per-shape comparison
   does not depend on the fit at all.

WHAT IS CLOSED, AND WHAT IS NOT
===============================
CLOSED: "32x32 was underutilizing the array" as an explanation for the conv2x loss. It
was underutilizing it, by about 23%, and the loss is 570%. The residual gap is the
kernel's steady-state throughput against VNNI, not the shape.

CLOSED: chained/standalone int8 ResNet bottleneck convolution on XDNA1 via mlir-aie, at
every spatial size this design can compile, loses to the Zen4 CPU by 5.7-11.4x. Two
independent designs (layers_conv2_x chained across 3 columns, ml/bottleneck standalone
on 1 column) now agree.

NOT CLOSED -- and these are real, not hedges:
  * Column count. Both measurements are 1-3 columns of a 4x5 array. The marginal-GOPS
    number is per-design, not per-chip; a design using all four columns could plausibly
    reach ~4x146 = 584 GOPS, which is still below the CPU's 819 but no longer 5.6x away.
    This is the one remaining lever that could change the arithmetic rather than the
    verdict.
  * Whether the AIE2 int8 MAC peak is being approached at all -- and the arithmetic says
    emphatically not. An AIE2 core does 256 int8 MACs/cycle; at 2 ops per MAC and 4
    compute cores in this column, one column is ~2 TOPS at 1 GHz. That figure is from the
    AIE2 architecture, NOT measured on this machine -- but against it, 146.1 GOPS is
    about 7% of a single column's peak. That is the same shape of finding as
    kernels/attention_bf16 (0.61 GFLOPS measured against 895, because it called aie::mmul
    zero times). Nobody has read conv2dk1.cc / conv2dk3.cc to check how they vectorize.
    If they leave the MAC array as idle as attention did, the 5.7x is a kernel-quality
    result and not a chip result, and it is the first thing to look at.
  * w > 32 for this op. Untested, because untestable without rewriting the design's
    buffering. 56x56 -- the shape that actually matters for ResNet50 -- is on the far
    side of that rewrite.

REPRODUCE
=========
  # NPU (ironenv). xclbinutil is NOT in ironenv/Scripts; the XRT SDK must be on PATH too
  # or aiecc dies at the final link with "tool 'xclbinutil' not found".
  export PATH=/c/Users/<user>/mlir-aie/ironenv/Scripts:/c/Xilinx/XRT/xrt_sdk/xrt:$PATH
  export PYTHONPATH=/c/Xilinx/XRT/xrt_sdk/xrt/python
  python3 kernels/bottleneck_sweep/sweep.py --iters 50 --warmup 3

  # CPU (resnet_env has torch + onnx + onnxruntime, so one activation covers it)
  conda activate resnet_env
  python kernels/bottleneck_sweep/cpu_sweep.py --iters 100 --warmup 10 \
      --workdir scratch/bottleneck_sweep

Nine aiecc JIT compiles on the first run (tens of minutes -- not separately timed);
later runs hit ~/.npu/cache. Run the two sides in the same sitting -- NPU latency on this
machine drifts between sessions independently of any code change.
